er polls show Pritzker leading by 18 to 20 points"], "rationale": "Polls since October show Pritzker ahead by 18 to 20 points, he is outspending Bailey roughly 25 to 1, and every major rater places the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column. A rematch in a state that leans six points Democratic, against an opponent already beaten by double digits, leaves Bailey without a visible path, supporting a Safe Democratic rating with a mid-teens expected margin.", "watch": "A late surge in Bailey's fundraising or a sharply nationalized Republican environment could narrow the margin, though an actual upset would require a historic polling failure.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent JB Pritzker (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Illinois' governor's race pits incumbent Democrat JB Pritzker against Republican Darren Bailey in a rematch of 2022, which Pritzker won by double digits. The state leans Democratic by about six points, and Pritzker enters the final stretch with an overwhelming financial edge, outspending Bailey roughly 25 to 1. Recent polls show him leading by 18 to 20 points, and every major race rater places the contest in the Solid or Safe Democratic column. Prediction markets price a Pritzker win at about 97 percent.\n\nBailey has pressed themes of cost of living and public safety in the campaign's only debate, and a third-party candidate, Collin Corbett, is on the ballot. Those threads have not visibly moved the race. Pritzker's decision to send money to other states' contests signals confidence in his own standing. The main question is the size of the margin, not the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.98, "margin": 13.88}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9829, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.983, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9789, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9753, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9721, "margin": 13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_de